 hotel  in a completely different outfit  followed quickly by eugene showing up to  him and also another shirt and he's in a  different outfit  giving him these bed sheets there was  weird feeling of it not feeling like a  realistic day in this episode and that  also goes to  where adam's at this cafe all day not  answering the phone calls of his father  but fia shows up just happens to answer  this cafe but decide to only go to her  family's hotel gathering  and pops up to say hi to carlo and then  goes to this cafe and  you're just like how is everyone getting  everywhere so quickly  and conveniently it did feel like there  were so  many locations and not enough  establishing shots either to kind of  catch your breath as the audience and  kind of realize  where are we in this day and i mean you  even have our lead michael start out in  court  then go home to deal with this threat  bring the dog to the animal hospital  then goes back  to court to work and then goes back to  the animal hospital  and then back home to adam while this  whole time adam was just preparing for  this insanely i guess long interview but  there's some  really intense suspense going on here  especially in the open where  getting blackmailed now i love he's  gotta go home  he sees there's the newspaper article  someone's telling him he knows adam did  it  he's got the bat and it really gets you  on the edge of your seat right away  there is some odd pacing issues with the  show though because the show will open  with that kind of pace  and then in particular this episode it  will just completely slow down in like a  scene with the cafe with adam and fia  there's not really a  great rhythm of this episode where it  has some really fast-paced scenes and  some really slow scenes and  it's not really totally all in sync and  like i'm saying it's like this giant  puzzle piece of a day you're trying to  kind of stay and figure out where you  are but what really stood out to me here  was the cinematography i think it  actually  stole the show for me in this episode i  love the cinematography just on the  scene with big mo on the porch  talking to eugene and this lighting they  had of the sun on her was just  really beautiful shot and they also do a  good job of showing  what a character's feeling with the  camera in particular in the cafe scene  with adam every time  fia even mentions her brother we get a  shallow focus  in the environment of adam and he's  clearly got some anxiety when that's  brought up so they really put you in the  mood of each character  usually adam michael in a scene with the  camera there's always funny details  though  i love shows dude that i always laugh at  like one in particular was when  the blackmailer texted michael when he's  at the hospital and  he wants 222 thousand dollars i would  love to just  like text back if i was michael can we  call it 220 000 does it need to be  exactly 222  such a random number like in the writers  room when they're deciding that like  who's where's the guy standing up  raising his hand and being like you know  what why don't we just make it 220 000.  why did you say 222. but clearly the  writing's on the wall here with adam and  fia's relationship it's almost becoming  this like  romeo and juliet story where they're now  in these  really bad separate camps that they  shouldn't even be talking  but they're actually starting to like  each other romantically here there is a  connection  and you kind of just roll your eyes at  adam constantly like how is this  smart to even be talking to a baxter but  maybe  it will be something that saves their  butt in the end because she seems like  the only sane baxter in this story so  they're kind of  making that character that can we can  kind of get some sensibility from that  angle of that family also i complained  before about the casting choice of  adam's teacher and that she looks too  similar to his age  i have another complaint in that  category when we 